#!/bin/sh
# pre-push: the half of the gate that CI cannot be.
#
# Where this lives now. The hook is at the REPO ROOT (core.hooksPath -> .githooks)
# and it runs ROOT scripts. It used to cd into desktop/ and call that package's
# npm scripts directly, which was one more way desktop acted as the root. The GUI
# boot smoke is still the desktop's work (it launches Electron), but the hook
# reaches it through the root now, the same way the root reaches every platform.
#
# The split, and why it is a split at all.
#
# CI covers the headless half on every push: the unit tests, the import audits
# (desktop/scripts/check-imports.js and check-package.js) and the iOS legs. What
# CI cannot run is the GUI boot smoke, because GitHub's macOS runners have no
# session to launch Electron into. That is the check that catches the fault which
# actually shipped: a build that passed every test and still opened a fatal
# "A JavaScript error occurred in the main process" dialog. A green unit-test run
# is not evidence the app starts.
#
# So the smoke is gated here, at the push, because a push to main IS the merge in
# this repo. Running it on every push rather than only on main keeps the rule
# simple: the push either passed the local gate or it did not.
#
# This hook is ADVISORY, and do not mistake it for the gate. `git push --no-verify`
# skips it, and a fresh clone has no hook until `npm run hooks:install` points
# core.hooksPath here. The unskippable gate is the CI run on the commit you pushed.
# Treat a green hook as the fast local signal, never as the only one.

set -e

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$root"

fail() {
  cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: ${1}

The local gate, from the repo root:
  npm test            every platform's tests through the root fan-out
  npm run check:imports   static import audit over the desktop source
  npm run smoke       boot the real Electron app and require a positive signal
  npm run check:package   audit the packaged artifact, when one exists

The smoke needs a real desktop session, which is why it lives here and not in
CI. Fix the failure and push again, or deliberately skip the gate with:

    git push --no-verify

Skipping is sometimes right (a docs-only push, a headless host). It is never
free: the smoke is the only check that proves the app still starts.
EOF
  exit 1
}

echo "pre-push: local gate (desktop unit tests, import audit, GUI boot smoke)"
if ! npm run verify; then
  fail "the verification suite failed."
fi

# A packaged build, if one is sitting there, is worth auditing on the way out:
# this is the check that catches a module the packer never copied, which is a
# fault the source tree cannot show. CI does this on the artifact it builds, so
# this is the local equivalent rather than a duplicate of CI's work.
if [ -d desktop/dist ]; then
  echo "pre-push: auditing the packaged build in desktop/dist/"
  if ! npm run check:package; then
    fail "the packaged-artifact audit failed."
  fi
fi

echo "pre-push: local gate passed."
